(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609111000-an-unchanged-oracle-is-the-control-for-a-regeneration")
  (:adr/topic "q9-regeneration-control")
  (:adr/title "An oracle that must NOT move is the control for a regeneration that moves everything else")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/com-abb-robotics"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110900-the-coercion-fixture-omitted-the-coerced-field"
                "adr-2609110800-a-generator-fallback-produced-a-selector-that-measured-nothing"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

`com-abb-robotics` was the last hand-written component in the cohort that had a
generator-derived alternative. Regenerating it puts it under the same derivation
as its siblings, so a future entity-table change cannot leave a fixture behind.

The difficulty with regenerating a MEASURED artifact is that its record quotes
values, and a regeneration moves them. A run in which every number changes is
indistinguishable from a run in which the measurement itself broke.

## The three signals, and why the middle one is the important one

    route count            30              unchanged
    oracle-float-divergence 172520021 ...  BYTE-IDENTICAL
    oracle-expand          all six moved

The float oracle is the control. It calls `coerce-field` with literal strings
and depends on no fixture, so a regeneration that only changes fixtures MUST
leave it exactly where it was. It did.

Had it moved too, the correct conclusion would not have been \"the fixtures
changed\" -- it would have been \"something about how I am measuring changed\",
and the expand numbers would have carried no information either way.

That is the general shape worth keeping: **when a change is expected to move
most of your numbers, the reading depends on having at least one number that
must not move.** Without it, a page of new values is not evidence of anything.

## What moved, and why it is still right

Every `oracle-expand` value moved, because the fixtures moved -- `seeded-row`
and `cred-row` now carry generated placeholders instead of the hand-chosen
\"Bracket\" and \"Flange\". The values are hashes of rendered records, so this is
expected.

The STRUCTURE is what the oracle is for, and it is unchanged:

    selectors 1, 3, 5   collapse to one floor
    selectors 0, 2, 4   each distinct from the floor and from each other

Selector 4 remains distinct from selector 0. That is the property that matters
for a two-ref entity, and it is precisely the property `com-accela` cannot have
-- which is why its regeneration removed the selector rather than keeping one
that duplicated another.

## What the regeneration gained

`fixture-data` selector 3 now carries `\"payloadKg\":\"1.5\"`. Robot coerces
`payloadKg` to `:float` and does not require it, so the generator's earlier
`:required`-only fixture omitted the one field the float arm exists to
exercise. The hand-written fixture had it because a person noticed. The
generator now derives it, so the next repository does not depend on someone
noticing again.

## Verification

`amu check :ok true`, `amu compile :ok true` at 237456 bytes (was 237083), the
artifact executed through `tools/kexe_loader.c` for every value quoted, and
parity unchanged: as-bool 9 of 9, page-limit 8 of 8, as-int 5 of 6 with the
cohort-wide `\"12abc\"` divergence.

## Cohort position

`com-accela` and `com-abb-robotics` are now generator-derived. `com-aadhaar` and
`com-aave` are the two originals and remain hand-written; they have not been
diffed against a generated candidate, so nothing here says whether their
fixtures carry the same gap the coercion fix closed. `com-aave` is the one to
look at first -- it declares coercions on unrequired fields across three
entities, more than any other pilot sampled.")))
